tions, which is \\((y-29)(y-45)(y-69)\\). Rewriting the equation with this common denominator, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{(y-45)(y-69) + (y-29)(y-69) - 2(y-29)(y-45)}{(y-29)(y-45)(y-69)} = 0.\n\\]\n\nSince the denominator cannot be zero, we focus on the numerator:\n\\[\n(y-45)(y-69) + (y-29)(y-69) - 2(y-29)(y-45) = 0.\n\\]\n\nExpanding each term, we have:\n\\[\n(y^2 - 114y + 3105) + (y^2 - 98y + 1981) - 2(y^2 - 74y + 1305) = 0.\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the expression inside the equation:\n\\[\ny^2 - 114y + 3105 + y^2 - 98y + 1981 - 2y^2 + 148y - 2610 = 0.\n\\]\n\nCombining like terms, we get:\n\\[\n(y^2 + y^2 - 2y^2) + (-114y - 98y + 148y) + (3105 + 1981 - 2610) = 0,\n\\]\n\\[\n0y^2 - 64y + 2476 = 0,\n\\]\n\\[\n-64y + 2476 = 0.\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(y\\), we get:\n\\[\n-64y = -2476,\n\\]\n\\[\ny = \\frac{2476}{64} = \\frac{619}{16}.\n\\]\n\nRecall that \\(y = x^2 - 10x\\). So, we substitute back to get:\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x = \\frac{619}{16}.\n\\]\n\nMultiplying through by 16 to clear the fraction, we get:\n\\[\n16x^2 - 160x = 619,\n\\]\n\\[\n16x^2 - 160x - 619 = 0.\n\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 16\\), \\(b = -160\\), and \\(c = -619\\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{160 \\pm \\sqrt{(-160)^2 - 4 \\cdot 16 \\cdot (-619)}}{2 \\cdot 16},\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{160 \\pm \\sqrt{25600 + 39616}}{32},\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{160 \\pm \\sqrt{65216}}{32},\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{160 \\pm 256}{32}.\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{160 + 256}{32} = \\frac{416}{32} = 13,\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{160 - 256}{32} = \\frac{-96}{32} = -3.\n\\]\n\nSince we are asked for the positive solution, we have:\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{13}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
